#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186082989 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f4bd2a8e-357a-5f22-bd50-608e68432a8d" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Critical Points of Polynomials"}] :question/id #uuid "5c0c0cf3-3d1c-5d6b-8835-d73c97e4cb39" :question/math-academy-id "e-6722" :question/problem "Find the critical points of the function $f(x)=2x^3-3x^2+5$." :question/worked-solution "A critical point of $f(x)$ is a point $x=c$ in the domain of $f$ such that either $f'(c)=0$ or $f'(c)$ does not exist.\n\nDifferentiating $f(x)$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf^{′}(x) &= \\frac{d}{dx}(2x^{3} - 3x^{2} + 5) \\\\\n&= 6x^{2} - 6x\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now proceed to find the critical points:\n\n- First, we compute the points where $f'(c) = 0$. We set the derivative equal to zero, and solve for $x$:\n$6x^{2} - 6x|= 0; 6x(x - 1)|= 0; x = 0, 1$\nTherefore, $f(x)$ has stationary points at $x=0$ and $x=1$.\n- Second, we note that $f'(x)$ exists for all values of $x$ in the domain of $f(x)$. So $f(x)$ has no non-stationary critical points.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the critical points of $f(x)$ are $x=0$ and $x=1$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186082990 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "48e49ade-8bfe-56e7-8b52-950fa84e4bb0" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Critical Points of Exponential Functions"}] :question/id #uuid "882080a6-fbb0-512f-ab9f-5a8ad0686317" :question/math-academy-id "e-6723" :question/problem "Find the critical points of the function $f(x) = e^{x^3-x}$." :question/worked-solution "A critical point of $f(x)$ is a point $x=c$ in the domain of $f$ such that either $f'(c)=0$ or $f'(c)$ does not exist.\n\nDifferentiating $f(x)$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf^{′}(x) &= \\frac{d}{dx}(e^{x^{3} - x}) \\\\\n&= (x^{3} - x)^{′}e^{x^{3} - x} \\\\\n&= (3x^{2} - 1)e^{x^{3} - x}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now proceed to find the critical points:\n\n- First, we compute the points where $f'(c) = 0$. We set the derivative equal to zero, and solve for $x$:\n$(3x^{2} - 1)e^{x^{3} - x}|= 0$\nSince $e^{x^3-x} \\neq 0$ for all $x$, we can divide this equation by $e^{x^3-x}$ and solve the resulting equation.\n$(3x^{2} - 1)e^{x^{3} - x}|= 0; 3x^{2} - 1|= 0; x|= ± \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}$\nTherefore, $f(x)$ has stationary points at $x=\\dfrac{\\sqrt 3}{3}$ and $x=-\\dfrac{\\sqrt 3}{3}$.\n- Second, we note that $f'(x)$ exists for all values of $x$ in the domain of $f(x)$. So $f(x)$ has no non-stationary critical points.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the critical points of $f(x)$ are\n\n$$\nx=\\dfrac{\\sqrt 3}{3}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nx=-\\dfrac{\\sqrt 3}{3}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083044 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "772ea6ab-9884-51ef-82df-a9d5cd523719" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Critical Points of Rational Functions"}] :question/id #uuid "a6af5246-ef2e-5b52-8ec7-2bc861b1398d" :question/math-academy-id "e-8757" :question/problem "What are the critical points of the function $f(x) = \\dfrac {x-3}{x^2 -6}$?" :question/worked-solution "A critical point of $f(x)$ is a point $x=c$ in the domain of $f$ such that either $f'(c)=0$ or $f'(c)$ does not exist.\n\nDifferentiating $f(x)$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf^{′}(x) &= \\frac{d}{dx}(\\frac{x - 3}{x^{2} - 6}) \\\\\n&= ((x - 3)^{′} \\cdot (x^{2} - 6) - (x - 3) \\cdot (x^{2} - 6)^{′})/((x^{2} - 6)^{2}) \\\\\n&= ((x^{2} - 6) - (x - 3) \\cdot 2x)/((x^{2} - 6)^{2}) \\\\\n&= (-6 + 6x - x^{2})/((x^{2} - 6)^{2}) \\\\\n&= - (x^{2} - 6x + 6)/((x^{2} - 6)^{2})\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now proceed to find the critical points:\n\n- First, we compute the points where $f'(c) = 0$. We set the derivative equal to zero, and solve for $x$ by completing the square:\n$- (x^{2} - 6x + 6)/((x^{2} - 6)^{2})|= 0; x^{2} - 6x + 6|= 0; x^{2} - 2 \\cdot 3x + 3^{2} - 3^{2} + 6|= 0; (x - 3)^{2} - 9 + 6|= 0; (x - 3)^{2} - 3|= 0; (x - 3)^{2}|= 3; x - 3|= ± \\sqrt{3}; x|= 3 ± \\sqrt{3}$.\nTherefore, $f(x)$ has stationary points at $x=3\\pm \\sqrt 3$.\n- Second, we note that $f'(x)$ does not exist at $x=\\pm \\sqrt 6$. But these points do not lie in the domain of $f(x)$. So $f(x)$ has no non-stationary critical points.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the critical points of $f(x)$ are\n\n$$\nx=3\\pm \\sqrt 3\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083051 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b5cda348-b3e8-5a89-abd4-bd6f3a7a8370" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Critical Points of Trigonometric Functions"}] :question/id #uuid "d8a14698-9de0-53ec-96b3-2ee20f14d5e5" :question/math-academy-id "e-9576" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x) = \\sqrt 3\\sin{2x} - \\cos{2x}$. Find critical points of $f(x)$ for $x \\in \\left(-\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}, \\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\right)$." :question/worked-solution "A critical point of $f(x)$ is a point $x=c$ in the domain of $f$ such that either $f'(c) = 0$ or $f'(c)$ does not exist.\n\nDifferentiating $f(x)$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf^{′}(x) &= \\frac{d}{dx}(\\sqrt{3}\\sin 2x - \\cos 2x) \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{3} \\cdot \\frac{d}{dx}(\\sin 2x) - \\frac{d}{dx}(\\cos 2x) \\\\\n&= 2\\sqrt{3}\\cos 2x + 2\\sin 2x\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now proceed to find the critical points:\n\n- First, we compute the points where $f'(c) = 0$. We set the derivative equal to zero, and solve for $x$:\n$2\\sqrt{3}\\cos 2x + 2\\sin 2x|= 0; 2\\sin 2x|=-2\\sqrt{3}\\cos 2x; \\frac{\\sin 2x}{\\cos 2x}|=-\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\cdot \\frac{\\cos 2x}{\\cos 2x}; \\tan 2x|=-\\sqrt{3}$\nThe general solution to our equation is\n$2x|= \\arctan (-\\sqrt{3}) + nπ; 2x|=-\\frac{π}{3} + nπ$.\nSolving for $x$, we get\n$x|=-\\frac{π}{6} + \\frac{nπ}{2}$.\nWe set $n=0, \\pm 1, \\pm 2,\\ldots$, until we have generated all solutions in the domain $-\\dfrac{\\pi}{2} \\lt x \\lt \\dfrac{\\pi}{2}$:setting $n=0$ gives $x = -\\dfrac{\\pi}{6}$setting $n=1$ gives $x = \\dfrac{\\pi}{3}$All other values of $n$ generate solutions outside the domain $-\\dfrac{\\pi}{2} \\lt x \\lt \\dfrac{\\pi}{2}$. So, the solutions to this equation for $x \\in \\left(-\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}, \\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\right)$ are\n$x =-\\dfrac{\\pi}{6}, \\qquad x = \\dfrac{\\pi}{3}$.\nTherefore, $f(x)$ has stationary points at $x =-\\dfrac{\\pi}{6}$ and $x = \\dfrac{\\pi}{3}$.\n- Second, we note that $f'(x)$ exists for all values of $x\\in \\left(-\\dfrac\\pi2, \\dfrac\\pi2\\right)$. So $f(x)$ has no non-stationary critical points.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the critical points of $f(x)$ for\n\n$$\nx\\in \\left(-\\dfrac\\pi2, \\dfrac\\pi2\\right)\n$$\n\nare\n\n$$\nx_1 =-\\dfrac{\\pi}{6}, \\qquad x_2 = \\dfrac{\\pi}{3}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083524 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f4bd2a8e-357a-5f22-bd50-608e68432a8d" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Critical Points of Polynomials"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c2c78ab6-b2a5-5fc9-8102-df0059fd902e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x =-1$ and $x = 1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186080638} {:answer/id #uuid "ef316532-efa8-50fc-bc38-4613ba578a8f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x = 1$ and $x = 4$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186080639} {:answer/id #uuid "194303dc-be8d-5457-856f-18cf5cfcdb23" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x =-1$ and $x = 4$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186080640} {:answer/id #uuid "4463d2ee-c670-5ed4-81e0-ad6ef7f0c4d9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x =-4$ and $x =-1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186080641} {:answer/id #uuid "66969161-9cda-5d4a-bab4-3f6de8206bfb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x =-4$ and $x = 1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186080642}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ef316532-efa8-50fc-bc38-4613ba578a8f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x = 1$ and $x = 4$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186080639} :answer-field/id #uuid "831eff03-6277-5b7f-9b25-50ae2e5b06e2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186080637}] :question/id #uuid "00fe70db-ae72-5c44-af16-89ddf096e915" :question/math-academy-id "q-21495" :question/problem "Find the critical points of the function $f(x) = 2x^{3} - 15x^{2} + 24x + 9$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083641 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "772ea6ab-9884-51ef-82df-a9d5cd523719" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Critical Points of Rational Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e6b2763f-1dc4-52a2-bd45-5220372c5263" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x = 0$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186081213} {:answer/id #uuid "ff94c620-29e9-5e70-9d2c-14ee19fa4ac9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x = 0$ and $x = 2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186081214} {:answer/id #uuid "040b9fdd-9a59-5fe4-802f-dd97d37afddc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x =-\\sqrt{2}$ and $x = 0$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186081215} {:answer/id #uuid "44fadb91-50e5-5837-9b9f-0cd247d561b4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x =-\\sqrt{2}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186081216} {:answer/id #uuid "7d2bef42-0410-5bca-9b21-e54a0af5f868" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x =-2$ and $x = 2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186081217}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7d2bef42-0410-5bca-9b21-e54a0af5f868" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x =-2$ and $x = 2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186081217} :answer-field/id #uuid "dbfb4a98-5054-58d1-88f3-28535864c817" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186081212}] :question/id #uuid "5947b13d-c33a-5e29-b427-e8d6513a809d" :question/math-academy-id "q-48741" :question/problem "What are the critical points of the function $f(x) = \\frac{x}{x^{2} + 4}$?"}]}
